+# Intent before presentation
+
+## Start with the user's task
+
+Identify what the audience needs to see, do, or conclude: observe a phenomenon, understand a mechanism, compare conditions, solve a problem, measure a quantity, explore a rule, or experience a visual effect. Infer a reasonable intent from supplied context; ask only when competing interpretations change the result materially. Do not turn a previous artifact's teaching goal into the current default.
+
+For a nontrivial request, sketch a small mapping in working notes:
+
+| Requested outcome | Relevant process / comparison | Visible evidence | Useful action |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| User-specific target | Which states matter | What lets the viewer establish the target | What reveals those states |
+
+This is a decision aid, not a required user-facing table or new approval round. A simple task may need one sentence. Exam conversion uses the conditional problem-to-animation reference.
+
+## Select representations deliberately
+
+- Use spatial motion and labels for position, direction, topology, contact, and causal order.
+- Use force vectors when they explain a balance, acceleration, constraint, or equation actually at issue. Preserve single-force vs resultant distinctions.
+- Use live numbers or boundary snapshots when a value, threshold, or before/after comparison matters. Separate source values, derived results, and illustrative values.
+- Show equations prominently when the user teaches or performs a derivation. Pair each equation with its system, interval, event side, or parameter branch; compact formulas are useful content, not unwanted prose.
+- Use a plot when its axes and trend answer the requested comparison, function, timing, extremum, or data question. Choose the relevant independent variable; time is not always useful. Omit plots when a trajectory, diagram, snapshot or equation already establishes the answer more directly.
+- Use energy displays only when energy transfer, conservation, loss, work, or an energy method supports the goal. An internally computed energy invariant is not a reason to show a dashboard.
+- Add a short observation cue only when it resolves an ambiguity that the scene and labels do not. An insight panel is optional.
+- Choose controls that reveal requested differences; do not add every model parameter or generic widget.
+
+The test for any proposed component is: what user question does this answer, and what useful evidence would disappear if it were removed? Remove or demote components with no concrete answer. Also identify omitted essentials; minimalism must not hide necessary givens, boundary values, mechanisms or complete equations.
+
+## Compose after selecting content
+
+Give the primary phenomenon sufficient scale. Allocate remaining area to the evidence the task actually needs. Use a side column, scene overlays, a comparison view, a bottom derivation strip, tabs, or an integrated instrument only when the content benefits from it. Readouts, plots, formulas and observation cues need not coexist. The layout references and bundled demo show composition and finish, not required regions or component counts.
+
+Assess comprehension against the user's task at meaningful states. Do not report teaching acceptance from numerical tests, control clickability, or attractive screenshots alone.
